    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The bedroom is more than just a place to sleep; it is a personal sanctuary, a place to recover and a space for reflection. To create a truly harmonious environment, two elements must work together: light and fabric. The interplay between the glow of a well-placed lamp and the soft diffusion of a linen curtain shapes the room’s mood, function and look.</p>
<p>Getting the balance right takes a layered approach. Whether you are aiming for a minimalist Scandinavian retreat or a rich, maximalist boudoir, understanding how to pair window treatments with light fittings is the key to a professional-looking result.</p>

<h2 id="layered-drapery">1. Layered drapery</h2>
<!--figure:symmetrical-panelled-bedroom|Full-length drapes frame the windows of this symmetrical bedroom, softening the tall upholstered wall panel and marble bedside tables.-->
<p>Layering curtains is one of the most effective ways to control both light and privacy throughout the day. With a double-rod system, you can combine a sheer fabric with a heavier, opaque drape. In the morning, the sheer layer lets soft, diffused daylight fill the room while keeping daytime privacy. At night, the heavier drapes can be closed for darkness and extra insulation. This “sandwich” approach adds depth to the window and creates a hotel-like feel that is both practical and visually striking.</p>

<h2 id="statement-chandeliers">2. Statement chandeliers</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-ring-chandelier-tufted-headboard|A double-ring chandelier sets the tone above a channel-tufted headboard in a calm beige bedroom.-->
<p>A chandelier is the jewellery of the bedroom. While bedrooms usually call for soft, relaxing light, a central overhead fitting provides the ambient light you need for dressing and cleaning. Choosing a statement piece, whether a mid-century modern mobile, a classic crystal design or a bohemian rattan pendant, sets the style for the whole room. When choosing a chandelier, make sure it is on a dimmer so you can move from practical lighting to a softer, low-glow atmosphere.</p>

<h2 id="recessed-cove-lighting">3. Recessed cove lighting</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-pendant-lights-cove-ceiling|A cove-lit ceiling casts a soft, indirect glow over this neutral bedroom, with globe pendants and sheer curtains below.-->
<p>For a minimalist or contemporary look, cove lighting is an excellent choice. By hiding LED strips in a ceiling tray or behind a pelmet above the curtains, the light bounces off the ceiling or walls rather than shining straight down. This reduces harsh shadows and creates a “floating” effect that can make the ceiling feel higher and the room more spacious. Paired with curtains, cove lighting can be fitted directly above the curtain track to create a glowing window effect that looks stunning after sunset.</p>

<h2 id="motorised-treatments">4. Motorised treatments</h2>
<p>In the age of the smart home, motorised curtains and blinds have moved from luxury novelty to practical staple. Motorisation lets you schedule your window treatments to open with the sunrise, and many people find waking to natural light a gentler start to the day. From a design point of view, motorised tracks remove the need for cords or wands, giving a cleaner, safer and more streamlined look. Many systems can now link with smart lighting, so a single “Sleep” scene can dim the lights and close the curtains at the same time.</p>

<h2 id="bedside-sconces">5. Bedside sconces</h2>
<p>Wall-mounted sconces are a great alternative to traditional bedside lamps, especially in smaller bedrooms where bedside table space is tight. Adjustable swing-arm sconces suit readers in particular, as they direct task light exactly where it is needed without disturbing a partner. For a polished look, consider hardwiring them during a renovation to hide the cables, or choose plug-in models with decorative cable covers that complement your colour palette.</p>

<h2 id="floor-lamps">6. Floor lamps</h2>
<p>A floor lamp is a useful tool for defining a particular zone in the bedroom, such as a reading nook or a dressing area. Because floor lamps take up vertical space, they draw the eye upward and can fill an empty corner that might otherwise feel neglected. Look for warm-toned shades, such as linen or parchment, to keep the light soft. Using a floor lamp alongside low-level bedside lighting creates a “triangulated” lighting scheme that makes the room feel cosy and balanced.</p>

<h2 id="smart-bulbs">7. Smart bulbs</h2>
<p>The technology inside the fitting matters as much as the fitting itself. Smart bulbs let you adjust the colour temperature of your bedroom through the day. In the morning, a cool white (around 5000K) can help you feel alert while getting ready. In the evening, shifting to a warm amber (around 2700K) echoes the sunset, and many people find warmer, dimmer light in the evening helps them wind down. Smart bulbs are also a simple, affordable way to add this flexibility to an existing lighting plan.</p>

<h2 id="velvet-curtains">8. Velvet curtains</h2>
<p>Velvet is a powerhouse material for bedroom curtains. Beyond its opulent appearance and rich texture, velvet is naturally heavy, which makes it good at softening sound and blocking light. If you live on a busy street or in a cold climate, velvet curtains help to reduce street noise and draughts. From a lighting point of view, the soft sheen of velvet interacts beautifully with low-level ambient light, creating gentle highlights and deep shadows that add a sense of mystery and luxury to the space.</p>

<h2 id="linen-sheers">9. Linen sheers</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-lit-headboard|Sheer curtains soften the window in this bedroom, working alongside a lit upholstered headboard and a pendant light.-->
<p>Linen sheers are the essence of effortless “California cool” or organic modern design. They bring a relaxed, tactile quality that softens the hard lines of a window frame. While they do not give full privacy at night, they are ideal for managing harsh midday sun, turning sharp glare into a soft, ethereal glow. Pairing linen sheers with wooden blinds or woven wood shades adds a second layer of texture that works well in coastal or rustic bedrooms.</p>

<h2 id="led-strips">10. LED strips</h2>
<!--figure:bedroom-panelled-headboard-cove-light|A backlit panelled headboard gives this soft neutral bedroom a gentle, indirect glow behind the bed.-->
<p>Often associated with gaming rooms, LED strips have been reimagined for the sophisticated bedroom. Used as accent lighting, they can highlight architectural features or furniture. Placing LED strips under the bed frame creates a “hovering” effect and can work as a motion-activated night light for moving around in the dark without waking a partner. Similarly, backlighting a headboard adds depth to the wall and provides a soft, indirect light source that is perfect for winding down before sleep.</p>

<h2 id="technical-considerations">Technical considerations for a flawless finish</h2>
<p>To get the combination of lighting and curtains just right, keep these professional tips in mind:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Colour temperature consistency:</strong> Make sure all your bulbs have a similar Kelvin rating. Mixing cool and warm bulbs in the same room can look jarring and uncoordinated. Aim for 2700K to 3000K for a restful bedroom.</li>
<li><strong>Curtain height and width:</strong> Hang curtains “high and wide”. Mount the rod as close to the ceiling as possible and extend it 15 to 25 cm (6 to 10 inches) beyond the window frame. This makes the window look larger and reduces light leaking in at the sides.</li>
<li><strong>The power of dimmer switches:</strong> Ideally every light in the bedroom should be dimmable. Dimmers do more than anything else to move the space from a practical daytime room to a relaxing night-time retreat.</li>
<li><strong>Fabric puddling:</strong> For a romantic, high-end look, let your curtains puddle 2.5 to 5 cm (1 to 2 inches) on the floor. For a modern, clean look, they should just kiss the floor or hover about 1 cm (half an inch) above it.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Check your scheme after dark as well as in daylight. Close the curtains, switch on each light in turn and adjust bulb warmth and dimmer levels until the room feels restful.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="bringing-it-together">Bringing it together</h2>
<p>A restful bedroom comes from treating light and fabric as one scheme rather than two separate decisions. Layer your curtains to manage daylight and privacy, layer your lighting so every task and mood is covered, and keep colour temperatures warm and consistent. With those foundations in place, the details, from a statement chandelier to a velvet drape, can bring the room to life.</p>
